<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/appraiser-services/">Services</a><span>/</span>High Wind Damage Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light fade-up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.06s">When Wind Takes the Roof, the Fence or the Cooler</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Wind in New Mexico arrives in several forms: steady spring gusts, sudden monsoon downbursts, and the occasional tornado on the eastern plains. Our New Mexico wind damage appraisal work sorts out what each storm actually did and what it costs to fix.</p><div class="btn-row fade-up" style="animation-delay:.22s;margin-top:26px"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn light" href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts fade-up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Peak seasons</small><b>Mar–May and Jul–Sep</b></div><div><small>Hazards</small><b>Gusts, microbursts, tornadoes</b></div><div><small>Typical damage</small><b>Roofing, fences, coolers</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Three kinds of wind loss</h2>
<h3>Spring wind season</h3>
<p>From roughly March through May, strong westerly winds sweep the state. Days of sustained gusts loosen shingles, peel back flat-roof edges, knock down block walls and wooden fences, and drive sand into window seals. Because the damage builds over several windy days, carriers sometimes question the date of loss or call the damage wear and tear.</p>
<h3>Monsoon microbursts</h3>
<p>The summer monsoon, generally July through September, brings thunderstorms that can collapse into downbursts. These columns of sinking air hit with concentrated force over a small area. One block in Las Cruces or Albuquerque may lose trees, carports and roof coverings while the next block is untouched, which can make neighbors’ claims look inconsistent even when they are not.</p>
<h3>Tornadoes on the eastern plains</h3>
<p>Tornadoes are less common here than on the plains farther east, but they do touch down in eastern New Mexico, near communities like Clovis, Portales, Roswell and Hobbs. Damage tends to be severe and patchy, with structural questions about framing, anchoring and manufactured-home tie-downs.</p>
<h2>Damage that drives wind disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creased and missing shingles.</strong> A creased shingle may still be lying flat but has lost its seal. Whether a few repairs or a full slope replacement is needed is the usual argument.</li>
<li><strong>Flat-roof edge and membrane lifts.</strong> Wind gets under parapet caps and coping and peels membranes. See <a href="/appraiser-services/flat-roof-stucco-appraisal/">flat roof and stucco appraisal</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Evaporative coolers.</strong> Coolers are blown off stands, and their roof penetrations then leak.</li>
<li><strong>Fences and walls.</strong> Coyote fences, block walls and wood fences are listed under other structures on most homeowners policies.</li>
<li><strong>Manufactured homes.</strong> Skirting, roof panels, siding and anchoring often need specialized parts and labor.</li>
<li><strong>Trees and debris.</strong> Tree removal coverage often has its own limits and conditions.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Documenting wind damage</h2>
<p>Good wind files tie the damage to a storm. Useful items include weather service reports or local storm data, photos taken soon after the event, neighbors’ observations, and debris patterns. We record the direction of lifted materials and look for signs like torn sealant strips and displaced fasteners that distinguish wind from age.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">A note on deductibles</b>Some New Mexico policies use a separate wind and hail deductible stated as a percentage of coverage. Our <a href="/claim-estimators/percentage-deductible-converter/">percentage deductible converter</a> shows how that compares with a flat-dollar deductible.</div>
<h2>No wind pool, but there is a FAIR plan</h2>
<p>New Mexico does not have a separate state wind pool. Owners who cannot find coverage in the regular market may obtain a policy through the New Mexico Property Insurance Program, the state’s FAIR plan. That policy has its own terms, so we read the actual form before assuming it contains an appraisal clause that works like a standard homeowners policy.</p>
<h3>Timing the date of loss</h3>
<p>Because spring wind events can stretch over several days, pinning down the date of loss helps everyone. A New Mexico wind damage appraisal file is stronger when it names the storm, shows when damage was first seen, and explains why it fits that event rather than a slow decline.</p>
<h2>How we help</h2>
<p>We inspect the property, measure the affected areas, separate wind damage from pre-existing conditions as fairly as the evidence allows, and price the repair with local labor and materials. If the same storm also brought hail, we cover both in one estimate; see <a href="/appraiser-services/hail-roof-appraisal/">hail and roof appraisal</a>. Owners in eastern towns such as <a href="/cities-we-serve/clovis/">Clovis</a> and <a href="/cities-we-serve/roswell/">Roswell</a> can find local notes on our city pages. Appraisal values the loss; it does not resolve whether wind or wear caused it as a coverage matter, so contested causation questions may also need a New Mexico attorney.</p>

<h2>Questions about high wind damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What if the carrier says my roof damage is just wear and tear?</summary><div class="a"><p>That is partly a causation question. The panel can value damage it finds, but if the dispute is purely about coverage, legal advice may be needed.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are fences covered under wind claims?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often under other structures coverage on a homeowners policy, subject to its limit. Your policy wording controls.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does my FAIR plan policy include appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many property forms include an appraisal condition, but you should read your actual New Mexico Property Insurance Program policy to confirm.</p></div></details></div>
